WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.660
An engineering workstation contains specialized software used to configure, program,

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.260
diagnose, and maintain control-system components.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:11.550
It may hold controller projects, HMI applications, device profiles, firmware tools,

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.925
network settings, and vendor utilities.

00:00:13.925 --> 00:00:16.040
Because it can make privileged operational changes,

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.710
it is one of the most security-sensitive assets in an ICS.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.225
Engineering work should follow approved pathways and change procedures.

00:00:22.225 --> 00:00:25.695
Dedicated accounts, controlled removable media, verified software,

00:00:25.695 --> 00:00:30.084
protected project repositories, and known-good backups help preserve integrity.

00:00:30.084 --> 00:00:32.840
Remote engineering access should be time-limited, monitored,

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.115
and tied to a specific work authorization.

00:00:35.115 --> 00:00:36.974
A historian performs a different role.

00:00:36.974 --> 00:00:41.535
It collects process values, alarms, events, and production information over time.

00:00:41.535 --> 00:00:44.960
Operators and engineers use trends for troubleshooting and performance analysis,

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.565
while business systems may consume selected data for reporting, planning,

00:00:48.565 --> 00:00:49.905
or quality functions.

00:00:49.905 --> 00:00:53.140
Historians are high-value because their data reveals how the process behaves.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:57.119
They also often connect operational and enterprise information flows.

00:00:57.119 --> 00:01:01.265
Architecture should avoid uncontrolled direct access from business networks into control

00:01:01.265 --> 00:01:02.006
systems.

00:01:02.006 --> 00:01:03.580
Replication, brokered interfaces,

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.200
or services in an industrial DMZ can reduce unnecessary paths.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.135
Access control should separate users who view data from users who administer collection,

00:01:11.135 --> 00:01:12.415
interfaces, or retention.

00:01:12.415 --> 00:01:16.680
Monitor changes to tags, collection sources, replication destinations, service identities,

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:17.880
and time settings.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.770
Protect historian availability and integrity so incident responders can trust the

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:22.735
timeline.

00:01:22.735 --> 00:01:26.345
Engineering workstations can change the process; historians can explain the process.

00:01:26.345 --> 00:01:29.965
Both deserve strong inventory, endpoint management, backup, logging,

00:01:29.965 --> 00:01:31.315
and recovery planning.

00:01:31.315 --> 00:01:34.530
Security teams should preserve their specialized functions rather than treating them as

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.240
interchangeable office computers.
